  "_source": "Curated skin tone for Hall of Famers the app has no picture of. 65% of players (20,945 of 32,038) carry no tone evidence at all -- no in-game portrait, no Madden-disc headshot, no Wikipedia photo, no CSV race -- so their tone is inferred from a position/era prior. That guess is wrong in both directions for individuals: the 1964 receiver prior leans light and made Bob Hayes white; the 1964 defensive back prior leans dark and made Paul Krause black. A prior cannot fix this, because moving it to catch one player breaks the players it currently gets right.",
  "_scope": "Hall of Famers only -- the players actually looked at, and the ones whose appearance is a matter of public record. 102 with no evidence at all, plus a short list whose one photograph is measured wrong. Everyone else stays inferred.",
  "_scale": "The app's 1-7 tone. 2 = light, 6 = dark. Deliberately coarse: these are recorded as 'which end of the scale', not a complexion reading, and the per-tone head pools supply the variation.",
  "_note": "The NFL had no black players from 1934 to 1945, so every pre-1946 draftee here is light by historical fact rather than by judgement.",
